> Part of the problem is that preparing software for a non-programming audience 
> is at least ten times harder, possibly 100 times harder, than hacking out 
> something that's useful to those willing to learn a bit about how these 
> things work.  People who don't work in programming have no inkling as to the 
> efforts needed to convert something a hacker can use into something the 
> general populace won't instantly break.  My parents, for example, would never 
> be able to use any of the lojban tools.  My wife, although non-technical, can 
> follow most of the instructions, but needs coaxing through some of the more 
> obscure parts, and if something goes wrong she doesn't know what to try next.
> 
> I have perhaps a dozen tools that I use daily, and colleagues use daily, that 
> would take months if not years to turn into consumer products.
> 
> > By the way, what's KDE?  I suspect something American.  Well,
> > I'm not an American and I assume we want non-American beginners too.
> 
> This is part of the problem.  Did you try using Google?
> 
>     http://www.google.co.uk/search?q=KDE
> 
> Top hit:
> 
>     " KDE is a powerful graphical desktop
>       environment for Unix workstations. "
> 
> In my version of an IT world people would at least try checking Google before 
> asking a question like that.  My wife would, my parents wouldn't.
> 
> While I'm extremely happy that you're enthusiastic about lojban and clearly 
> interested in helping, I have no insight into the mindset of a person who 
> wouldn't use Google to research that sort of question before coming straight 
> back out and asking.
> 
> Without people like you this project will forever remain an obscure, cultish, 
> techie-only backwater..  You're absolutely right that for lojban eventually to 
> expand and become more mainstream, it's people like you we need to cater for.  
> 
> But I have no idea how to do that.
